      "problem": "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(8p,-q)$ and $(-2p,-9q).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(8p,-q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-2p,-9q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p+(-2p)}{2},\\frac{-q+(-9q)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p-2p}{2},\\frac{-q-9q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6p}{2},\\frac{-10q}{2}) \\\\  & =(3p,-5q).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
