    {
      "dom_id": "question-216947",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246979/assets/226b832db682da8379d9567123974abb3d44c02f4db3c3ef78d48c1a8f2a491e.png)\n\nGiven that $(1,1)$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "The given points are ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(-2,0)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(1,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (1,1) & =(\\frac{a-2}{2},\\frac{b+0}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\frac{a-2}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}a=4 \\\\ b=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=4$ and $b=2.$",
